/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An FRP is a matrix, usually epoxy, holding long thin fibres of carbon, glass or aramid.
   - With all fibres in one direction, the ply has three perpendicular planes of symmetry: it is orthotropic.
   - Ef and Em are the fibre and matrix moduli (GPa); Vf and Vm = 1 − Vf the volume fractions.
   - The inverse rule is rough, and fails for carbon, whose fibres are soft across their length; measured values are used.
   - In its own axes the ply's stress–strain law has no coupling between normal and shear terms:
   - σ1, σ2 are the normal stresses along and across the fibres and τ12 the in-plane shear stress (GPa or MPa); ε1, ε2 the normal strains and γ12 the engineering shear strain.
   - The Q terms are the reduced stiffnesses, in the unit of E; ν21 = ν12E2/E1 is the minor Poisson's ratio.
   - The relation ν12/E1 = ν21/E2 keeps the matrix symmetric, so ν21 is always the smaller ratio.
   - In a panel the plies lie at angles.
   - Q̄11 is the stiffness along x; Q̄16 couples direct stress along x to shear strain.
*/
