/* book Worked example 2: Q: A carbon/epoxy ply has E1 = 140 GPa, E2 = 10 GPa, ν12 = 0.30 and G12 = 5 GPa. Find ν21 and the Q matrix. Then find Q̄11, Q̄12, Q̄66, Q̄16 and the modulus Ex for the ply turned to θ = 45°.
   1. ν21 = 0.30 × 10/140 = 0.0214; ν12ν21 = 0.00643; 1 − ν12ν21 = 0.99357.
   2. Q11 = 140/0.99357 = 140.91 GPa; Q22 = 10/0.99357 = 10.06 GPa; Q12 = 3/0.99357 = 3.02 GPa; Q66 = 5.00 GPa.
   3. Check: Q11 − Q12²/Q22 = 140.91 − 0.91 = 140.00 GPa = E1.
   4. At 45°: m = n = 0.7071, so m⁴ = n⁴ = m²n² = m³n = mn³ = 0.25.
   5. Q̄11 = 0.25 × [140.91 + 2 × (3.02 + 10) + 10.06] = 44.25 GPa.
   6. Q̄12 = 0.25 × (140.91 + 10.06 − 20) + 0.5 × 3.02 = 34.25 GPa; Q̄66 = 0.25 × (140.91 + 10.06 − 6.04 − 10) + 0.5 × 5 = 36.23 GPa.
   7. Q̄16 = 0.25 × [(140.91 − 3.02 − 10) + (3.02 − 10.06 + 10)] = 0.25 × (140.91 − 10.06) = 32.71 GPa: strong extension–shear coupling.
   8. Modulus along x of the free ply: 1/Ex = m⁴/E1 + (1/G12 − 2ν12/E1)m²n² + n⁴/E2 = 0.25 × (1/140 + 1/5 − 0.6/140 + 1/10) = 0.07571 GPa⁻¹, Ex = 13.2 GPa.
   Answer: ν21 = 0.0214; Q11 = 140.91, Q22 = 10.06, Q12 = 3.02, Q66 = 5.00 GPa. At 45°: Q̄11 = 44.25, Q̄12 = 34.25, Q̄66 = 36.23, Q̄16 = 32.71 GPa, and Ex = 13.2 GPa, a tenth of E1. */
