/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Describe a laminate by its stacking sequence, the ply angles from one face to the other, such as [0/90/90/0], written [0/90]s because it is symmetric about the mid-plane.
   - Classical laminate theory makes the Kirchhoff assumption for the whole stack: the strain at height z is the mid-plane strain plus z times the curvature, ε = ε⁰ + zκ.
   - Aij is the extensional stiffness (N/mm); Bij the coupling stiffness (N); Dij the bending stiffness (N·mm); Q̄ in N/mm² (1 GPa = 1000 N/mm²); z in mm; i, j = 1, 2, 6.
   - A weights every ply by its thickness, so it does not depend on the order of the plies.
   - N = (Nx, Ny, Nxy) are the in-plane forces per unit width (N/mm); M = (Mx, My, Mxy) the moments per unit width (N·mm/mm); ε⁰ the mid-plane strains; κ the curvatures (mm⁻¹).
   - In a symmetric laminate every ply at +z has a twin at −z, their contributions to B cancel, and B = 0: N = Aε⁰ and M = Dκ, so stretching and bending are independent.
   - A balanced laminate has a −θ ply for every +θ ply, which makes A16 = A26 = 0 so that in-plane loads cause no shear.
   - A laminate strip of width b bent about an axis across it behaves as a beam only if we say what happens across the width.
   - A narrow beam is free to curl sideways, so My = 0 instead.
   - EI is the bending stiffness of a narrow symmetric laminate beam (N·mm²); b its width (mm); D11, D12, D22 the bending stiffnesses per unit width (N·mm).
*/
