/* book Worked example: Q: A [0/90]s laminate has four carbon/epoxy plies, each 0.125 mm thick, with Q11 = 140.91 GPa, Q22 = 10.06 GPa, Q12 = 3.02 GPa and Q66 = 5.00 GPa. Find A11, D11, D12 and D22, the value of B, and the bending stiffness of a 100 mm wide strip held flat across and free to curl.
   1. Total thickness 0.5 mm. Interfaces from the mid-plane: z = −0.25, −0.125, 0, 0.125, 0.25 mm. Plies: 0° outside, 90° inside. For a 90° ply Q̄11 = Q22 and Q̄22 = Q11; Q̄12 = Q12 for both.
   2. A11 = 2 × 140.91 × 0.125 + 2 × 10.06 × 0.125 = 37.743 GPa·mm = 37,743 N/mm.
   3. Cube differences: an outer ply has 0.25³ − 0.125³ = 0.013672 mm³, an inner ply 0.125³ − 0 = 0.001953 mm³ (two of each).
   4. D11 = ⅓ × 2 × (140.91 × 0.013672 + 10.06 × 0.001953) = 1.2974 GPa·mm³ = 1297.4 N·mm.
   5. D22 = ⅓ × 2 × (10.06 × 0.013672 + 140.91 × 0.001953) = 275.2 N·mm; D12 = 3.02 × 0.5³/12 × 1000 = 31.45 N·mm.
   6. B = 0: each ply at +z has an identical ply at −z, and their (zk² − zk−1²) terms are equal and opposite.
   7. Held flat across: EI = bD11 = 100 × 1297.4 = 129,740 N·mm².
   8. Free to curl: EI = 100 × (1297.4 − 31.45²/275.2) = 100 × (1297.4 − 3.59) = 129,381 N·mm², 0.28% less. D12 is small for a cross-ply, so the two limits are close.
   Answer: A11 = 37,743 N/mm; D11 = 1297.4, D22 = 275.2, D12 = 31.45 N·mm; B = 0; EI = 129,740 N·mm² held flat and 129,381 N·mm² free. */
