/* book Worked example 2: Q: A two-ply [0/90] laminate (0° ply below, 90° ply above, each 0.125 mm, same plies) is pulled by Nx = 10 N/mm with no moment. Find A11, B11 and D11, then solve the full laminate equations for the mid-plane strain and the curvature.
   1. Interfaces: z = −0.125, 0, 0.125 mm.
   2. A11 = (140.91 + 10.06) × 0.125 = 18,871 N/mm.
   3. B11 = ½[140.91 × (0 − 0.015625) + 10.06 × (0.015625 − 0)] × 1000 = −1,022.2 N; B22 = 1,022.2 N. The laminate is unsymmetric, so B ≠ 0.
   4. D11 = ⅓(140.91 + 10.06) × 0.125³ × 1000 = 98.29 N·mm.
   5. Solving the 6 × 6 system [A B; B D][ε⁰; κ] = [10, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0] (with A12 = 754.9, A22 = 18,871 N/mm and D12 = 3.932, D22 = 98.29 N·mm): εx⁰ = 1.22 × 10⁻³, κx = 1.27 × 10⁻² mm⁻¹, and κy is zero to rounding.
   6. The in-plane pull bends the strip to a radius of about 1/|κx| = 79 mm. A laminate with B = 0 would stay flat with εx⁰ close to Nx/A11 = 5.30 × 10⁻⁴; with coupling the effective in-plane stiffness A − BD⁻¹B is lower, so the mid-plane strain is more than doubled.
   7. Check of the first row: A11εx⁰ + A12εy⁰ + B11κx + B12κy = 10.000 N/mm, the applied 10 N/mm.
   Answer: A11 = 18,871 N/mm, B11 = −1,022 N, D11 = 98.29 N·mm; εx⁰ = 1.22 × 10⁻³ and κx = 1.27 × 10⁻² mm⁻¹: pulling the unsymmetric laminate bends it. */
